{% extends 'guides/how_long_by_pieces/_layout.html.twig' %}

{% block size_intro %}
    <p>
        300 pieces is the one-sitting puzzle: long enough to feel like a real puzzle, short enough to finish before
        dinner. It is also a popular practice size among speed puzzlers — big enough to rehearse the whole routine of
        flipping, sorting and building colour blocks, small enough to run it again the same week.
    </p>
{% endblock %}

{% block size_callout %}
    <div class="guide-callout">
        <p>
            {% if distribution.p75Seconds < 3600 %}
                Three out of four recorded 300-piece solves finish within {{ distribution.p75Seconds|compactTime }}, so
                most solves at this size stay under an hour.
            {% else %}
                Half of all recorded 300-piece solves finish within {{ distribution.medianSeconds|compactTime }}.
            {% endif %}
            If yours took much longer, look at the piece size first: many 300-piece puzzles for adults are made with
            large pieces, which are easy on the eyes but spread over a lot more table.
        </p>
    </div>
{% endblock %}

{% block size_factors %}
    <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
            <strong>Large-piece formats.</strong> A large-piece 300 is a comfortable puzzle, not a fast one: more reaching,
            fewer pieces within view at once. Compare times only between puzzles of a similar finished size.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A practised routine.</strong> The same three steps — everything face-up, edges out, colour groups —
            decide a 500 or a 1000 too, and at 300 pieces you can rehearse them in about
            {{ distribution.medianSeconds|compactTime }}. Get them smooth here and the bigger sizes follow.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Colour blocks over single pieces.</strong> At 300 pieces the picture usually splits into a handful
            of clear areas. Building each area as its own small puzzle beats hunting for one specific piece.
        </li>
    </ul>
{% endblock %}
